here's a fun geometry problem we have a  circle we have a couple of tangent line  segments with length equal to one and we  have a couple of golden triangles with  area equal to one the question is to  solve for the radius of the circle to  get started suppose the other leg has  length equal to X then since its area is  equal to one we have x times one over  two is equal to one which means X is  equal to two  from here we can solve for the  hypotenuse of the triangle it'll be the  square root of one squared plus two  squared which is equal to the square  root of five now let R be the radius of  the circle we can now focus on this  right triangle and we must have that r  squared plus the square of one plus  square root of 5 is equal to the square  of R plus two we can expand each  binomial and then cancel out the r  squared terms to get that 4r is equal to  two plus two root 5. this means R is  equal to one plus root 5 all over two  which is equal to the golden ratio which  is approximately 1.618
 

